<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<usx version="3.0">
  <book code="JAS" style="id">JAMES</book>
  <chapter number="1" style="c" sid="JAS 1"/>
  <para style="s1">Greeting</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="JAS 1:1"/>
    James, a servant wholly devoted to God and to the Lord Jesus Christ,
    <verse eid="JAS 1:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">To the twelve tribes living in dispersion among the nations:</para>
  <para style="p">Greetings.</para>
  <para style="s1">Trials of Faith</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="JAS 1:2"/>
    Count it as complete joy, my brothers and sisters, whenever you are confronted with trials of many kinds,
    <verse eid="JAS 1:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="JAS 1:3"/>
    because you recognise that the testing of your faith brings about steadfast endurance.
    <verse eid="JAS 1:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="JAS 1:4"/>
    Let endurance be allowed to reach its full effect, so that you may become mature and whole, lacking nothing required for spiritual completeness.
    <verse eid="JAS 1:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="JAS 1:5"/>
    If any of you is lacking wisdom, he should ask God, who gives generously to all without reproach, and it will be given to him.
    <verse eid="JAS 1:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="JAS 1:6"/>
    But he must ask in faith, without inner hesitation, for the one who wavers is like a wave of the sea, driven and tossed about by the wind.
    <verse eid="JAS 1:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="JAS 1:7"/>
    Such a person should not expect to receive anything from the Lord,
    <verse eid="JAS 1:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="JAS 1:8"/>
    being divided in allegiance and unstable in all his ways.
    <verse eid="JAS 1:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="JAS 1:9"/>
    Let the brother who is in humble circumstances rejoice in the dignity God has given him,
    <verse eid="JAS 1:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="JAS 1:10"/>
    and let the wealthy rejoice in being brought low, because he will pass away like a flower of the field.
    <verse eid="JAS 1:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="JAS 1:11"/>
    For the sun rises with scorching heat and dries up the grass; its flower falls, and its outward beauty is destroyed. In the same way, the wealthy person will fade away in the midst of his pursuits.
    <verse eid="JAS 1:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="JAS 1:12"/>
    Blessed is the man who remains steadfast under trial, because once he has been approved, he will receive the crown of life which God has promised to those who love Him.
    <verse eid="JAS 1:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Temptations to Sin</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="JAS 1:13"/>
    Let no one who is being tempted say, “I am being tempted by God,” for God is untouched by evil, and He Himself tempts no one.
    <verse eid="JAS 1:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="JAS 1:14"/>
    Rather, each person is tempted when he is drawn away and enticed by his own desire.
    <verse eid="JAS 1:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="JAS 1:15"/>
    Then desire, after it has conceived, gives birth to sin; and sin, when it reaches full maturity, brings forth death.
    <verse eid="JAS 1:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Good Giving and Perfect Gift from God</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="JAS 1:16"/>
    Do not be led astray, my beloved brothers and sisters.
    <verse eid="JAS 1:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="JAS 1:17"/>
    Every good act of giving and every perfect gift comes down from above, descending from the Father of the heavenly lights, with whom there is no change or shifting shadow.
    <verse eid="JAS 1:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="JAS 1:18"/>
    In accordance with His own purpose, He brought us into life through the word of truth, so that we would be a kind of firstfruits among all He has created.
    <verse eid="JAS 1:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Hearing and Doing God’s Word</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="JAS 1:19"/>
    Be sure of this, my beloved brothers and sisters: Let every person be quick to listen, slow to speak, and slow to anger,
    <verse eid="JAS 1:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="JAS 1:20"/>
    for human anger does not bring about the righteousness God desires.
    <verse eid="JAS 1:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="JAS 1:21"/>
    Therefore, put away all moral impurity and the excess of wickedness, and humbly receive the word that has been planted within you, which is able to save your souls.
    <verse eid="JAS 1:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="JAS 1:22"/>
    But become people who put the word into practice, not merely those who listen and deceive themselves.
    <verse eid="JAS 1:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="JAS 1:23"/>
    For if anyone hears the word but does not act on it, he is like a man who looks at his natural face in a mirror;
    <verse eid="JAS 1:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="JAS 1:24"/>
    he looks at himself, goes away, and immediately forgets what kind of person he was.
    <verse eid="JAS 1:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="JAS 1:25"/>
    But the one who carefully looks into the perfect law that brings freedom, and continues in it, not as a forgetful hearer but as one who acts on what he has learned, this one will be blessed in what he does.
    <verse eid="JAS 1:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Pure and Undefiled Religion</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="JAS 1:26"/>
    If anyone claims to practise religion but does not keep his tongue under control, he is only deceiving himself; such religion has no real substance.
    <verse eid="JAS 1:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="JAS 1:27"/>
    Religion that God the Father recognises as pure and uncorrupted is shown in practical compassion for orphans and widows in their suffering, together with a life that remains untainted by the world.
    <verse eid="JAS 1:27"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="JAS 1"/>
  <chapter number="2" style="c" sid="JAS 2"/>
  <para style="s1">Favouritism in the Church</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="JAS 2:1"/>
    My brothers and sisters, as those who hold faith in our glorious Lord Jesus Christ, you must not practise partiality.
    <verse eid="JAS 2:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="JAS 2:2"/>
    For imagine that a man enters your assembly wearing gold rings and elegant clothing, and at the same time a poor person enters wearing worn and tattered garments.
    <verse eid="JAS 2:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="JAS 2:3"/>
    If you give preferential attention to the one dressed in fine clothes and say, “Sit here in a place of honour,” while you say to the poor person, “Stand over there,” or, “Sit down at my feet,”
    <verse eid="JAS 2:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="JAS 2:4"/>
    have you not made distinctions among yourselves and become judges whose reasoning is shaped by corrupt motives?
    <verse eid="JAS 2:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="JAS 2:5"/>
    Listen carefully, my beloved brothers and sisters: Has not God chosen those who are poor by the standards of this world to be rich in faith and heirs of the kingdom He promised to those who love Him?
    <verse eid="JAS 2:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="JAS 2:6"/>
    Yet you have dishonoured the poor. Are not the wealthy the very ones who exploit you and drag you before the courts?
    <verse eid="JAS 2:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="JAS 2:7"/>
    Are they not the ones who speak with contempt against the honourable name by which you have been called?
    <verse eid="JAS 2:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="JAS 2:8"/>
    If you are truly carrying out the royal law according to Scripture, “You shall love your neighbour as yourself,” you are doing what is right.
    <verse eid="JAS 2:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="JAS 2:9"/>
    But if you show favouritism, you commit sin and are convicted by the Law as transgressors.
    <verse eid="JAS 2:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="JAS 2:10"/>
    For whoever keeps the whole Law but stumbles at a single point has become accountable for all of it.
    <verse eid="JAS 2:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="JAS 2:11"/>
    For the One who said, “You shall not commit adultery,” also said, “You shall not murder.” If you do not commit adultery but do commit murder, you have become a violator of the Law.
    <verse eid="JAS 2:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="JAS 2:12"/>
    Speak and act as people who are to be judged by the law that brings freedom.
    <verse eid="JAS 2:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="JAS 2:13"/>
    For judgment will be without mercy towards the one who has shown no mercy; mercy triumphs over judgment.
    <verse eid="JAS 2:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Faith Without Works Is Dead</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="JAS 2:14"/>
    What benefit is it, my brothers and sisters, if someone claims to have faith but does not demonstrate it through works? Can such faith save him?
    <verse eid="JAS 2:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="JAS 2:15"/>
    If a brother or sister is without adequate clothing and lacks daily nourishment,
    <verse eid="JAS 2:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="JAS 2:16"/>
    and one of you says to them, “Go in peace; stay warm and eat well,” but you do not give them what their bodies need, what benefit is that?
    <verse eid="JAS 2:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="JAS 2:17"/>
    In the same way, faith by itself, if it does not express itself in action, is dead.
    <verse eid="JAS 2:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="JAS 2:18"/>
    But someone may object, “You have faith, and I have works.” Show me your faith apart from works, and I will show you my faith by what I do.
    <verse eid="JAS 2:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="JAS 2:19"/>
    You believe that God is one; you do well. Even the demons believe this, and they shudder.
    <verse eid="JAS 2:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="JAS 2:20"/>
    Do you want to be shown, you foolish person, that faith without works is barren?
    <verse eid="JAS 2:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="JAS 2:21"/>
    Was not Abraham our father justified or shown to be righteous by works when he offered his son Isaac on the altar?
    <verse eid="JAS 2:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="JAS 2:22"/>
    You see that his faith was working together with his actions, and by his actions faith was brought to its intended completion.
    <verse eid="JAS 2:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="JAS 2:23"/>
    In this way the Scripture was fulfilled which says, “Abraham believed God, and it was credited to him as righteousness,” and he was called a friend of God.
    <verse eid="JAS 2:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="JAS 2:24"/>
    You see that a person is justified or shown to be righteous by works and not by faith that remains alone.
    <verse eid="JAS 2:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="JAS 2:25"/>
    In the same manner, was not Rahab the prostitute also justified or shown to be righteous by works when she received the messengers and sent them out by another way?
    <verse eid="JAS 2:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="JAS 2:26"/>
    For just as the body without the spirit is dead, so faith without works is dead.
    <verse eid="JAS 2:26"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="JAS 2"/>
  <chapter number="3" style="c" sid="JAS 3"/>
  <para style="s1">Restraining the Tongue</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="JAS 3:1"/>
    Not many of you should aspire to be teachers, my brothers and sisters, because you know that those who teach will be evaluated with greater strictness.
    <verse eid="JAS 3:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="JAS 3:2"/>
    For all of us fail in many respects. If someone does not fail in speech, he is a fully mature man, able also to exercise control over his whole body.
    <verse eid="JAS 3:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="JAS 3:3"/>
    When we place a bit into the mouth of a horse so that it will submit to us, we direct the movement of its entire body.
    <verse eid="JAS 3:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="JAS 3:4"/>
    Consider ships as well: Although they are very large and driven by strong winds, they are guided by a very small rudder wherever the helmsman intends.
    <verse eid="JAS 3:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="JAS 3:5"/>
    In the same way, the tongue is a small part of the body, yet it boasts of great accomplishments. See how a tiny spark can set an enormous forest on fire.
    <verse eid="JAS 3:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="JAS 3:6"/>
    The tongue is also a fire, a sphere of unrighteousness. The tongue is set among our bodily members as something that corrupts the whole person, ignites the entire course of life, and is itself set on fire by hell.
    <verse eid="JAS 3:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="JAS 3:7"/>
    For every kind of animal, bird, reptile, and sea creature is being tamed and has been tamed by mankind,
    <verse eid="JAS 3:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="JAS 3:8"/>
    but no human being can tame the tongue. It is restless and destructive, saturated with deadly poison.
    <verse eid="JAS 3:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="JAS 3:9"/>
    With it we praise our Lord and Father, and with it we speak curses against men who have been made according to the likeness of God.
    <verse eid="JAS 3:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="JAS 3:10"/>
    From the same mouth come both praise and cursing. My brothers and sisters, this ought not to be so.
    <verse eid="JAS 3:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="JAS 3:11"/>
    Does a spring send out fresh water and bitter water from the same opening?
    <verse eid="JAS 3:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="JAS 3:12"/>
    Can a fig tree, my brothers and sisters, produce olives, or a grapevine produce figs? Neither can salt water yield fresh water.
    <verse eid="JAS 3:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Two Kinds of Wisdom</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="JAS 3:13"/>
    Who among you is wise and understanding? Let him show it by a life marked by good conduct, displaying deeds done in the humility that comes from wisdom.
    <verse eid="JAS 3:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="JAS 3:14"/>
    But if you cherish bitter jealousy and self-centred ambition in your heart, do not boast and do not deny the truth.
    <verse eid="JAS 3:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="JAS 3:15"/>
    This kind of wisdom does not come down from above, but is earthly, unspiritual, and demonic.
    <verse eid="JAS 3:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="JAS 3:16"/>
    For where jealousy and self-seeking ambition are present, disorder and every kind of corrupt practice will follow.
    <verse eid="JAS 3:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="JAS 3:17"/>
    But the wisdom that comes from above is first pure, then peace-loving, gentle, reasonable and teachable, full of mercy and good fruits, free from favouritism and hypocrisy.
    <verse eid="JAS 3:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="JAS 3:18"/>
    And a harvest of righteousness is sown in peace by those who actively pursue peace.
    <verse eid="JAS 3:18"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="JAS 3"/>
  <chapter number="4" style="c" sid="JAS 4"/>
  <para style="s1">Against Worldliness</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="JAS 4:1"/>
    From where do quarrels arise, and from where do conflicts come among you? Do they not originate from your cravings that wage war within your inner being?
    <verse eid="JAS 4:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="JAS 4:2"/>
    You desire intensely but remain empty-handed. You murder—whether through actual bloodshed or through the hatred in your hearts that God counts as murder—and you covet, burning with jealous longing for what belongs to others, yet you still cannot obtain these things. You quarrel and fight. You lack because you do not ask God.
    <verse eid="JAS 4:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="JAS 4:3"/>
    When you do ask, you do not receive, because you ask with corrupt motives, intending to spend it on self-indulgent pleasures.
    <verse eid="JAS 4:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="JAS 4:4"/>
    You unfaithful people, do you not understand that friendship with the world means opposition towards God? Therefore, anyone who chooses to align himself with the world sets himself against God as an enemy.
    <verse eid="JAS 4:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="JAS 4:5"/>
    Or do you think the Scripture speaks without meaning when it says that God yearns with jealous concern over the spirit He has caused to dwell within us?
    <verse eid="JAS 4:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="JAS 4:6"/>
    But He provides an even greater measure of grace. For this reason Scripture says, “God opposes the proud, but gives grace to the humble.”
    <verse eid="JAS 4:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="JAS 4:7"/>
    Therefore, place yourselves under God’s authority. Take a firm stand against the devil, and he will flee from you.
    <verse eid="JAS 4:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="JAS 4:8"/>
    Draw near to God, and He will draw near to you.
    <verse eid="JAS 4:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">A Call to Sinners to Humble Themselves Before God</para>
  <para style="p">Cleanse your hands, you sinners, and purify your hearts, you who are divided in loyalty.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="JAS 4:9"/>
    Recognise your misery, grieve, and weep. Let your laughter be turned into mourning and your joy into sorrow.
    <verse eid="JAS 4:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="JAS 4:10"/>
    Lower yourselves before the Lord, and He will raise you up.
    <verse eid="JAS 4:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Not Speaking Against One Another</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="JAS 4:11"/>
    Do not speak against one another, brothers and sisters. The one who speaks against a brother or passes judgment on his brother speaks against the law and judges the law. If you judge the law, you are not living under the law but setting yourself up as its judge.
    <verse eid="JAS 4:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="JAS 4:12"/>
    There is only one Lawgiver and Judge, the One who is able to save and to destroy. Who are you to pass judgment on your neighbour?
    <verse eid="JAS 4:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Boasting About Tomorrow</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="JAS 4:13"/>
    Now listen, you who say, “Today or tomorrow we will go to this or that city, spend a year there, conduct business, and make a profit.”
    <verse eid="JAS 4:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="JAS 4:14"/>
    You do not know what your life will be like tomorrow. What is your life? You are a mist that appears for a short time and then disappears.
    <verse eid="JAS 4:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="JAS 4:15"/>
    Instead, you ought to say, “If the Lord wills, we will live and carry out this or that plan.”
    <verse eid="JAS 4:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="JAS 4:16"/>
    But as it is, you take pride in your self-confidence. All such boasting is evil.
    <verse eid="JAS 4:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="JAS 4:17"/>
    Therefore, for the one who knows what is right to do and does not do it, for that person it is sin.
    <verse eid="JAS 4:17"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="JAS 4"/>
  <chapter number="5" style="c" sid="JAS 5"/>
  <para style="s1">Judgment of Rich Oppressors</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="JAS 5:1"/>
    Come now, you who are wealthy: Grieve aloud and lament because of the calamities that are coming upon you.
    <verse eid="JAS 5:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="JAS 5:2"/>
    Your wealth has rotted away, and your fine garments have become food for moths.
    <verse eid="JAS 5:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="JAS 5:3"/>
    Your gold and silver have become corroded, and their corrosion will stand as evidence against you and consume your flesh like fire. You have hoarded riches in what are the last days.
    <verse eid="JAS 5:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="JAS 5:4"/>
    Look closely: The wages you unjustly withheld from the labourers who reaped your fields are crying out for justice, and the cries of the harvesters have reached the ears of the Lord of armies.
    <verse eid="JAS 5:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="JAS 5:5"/>
    You have lived on the earth in indulgence and luxury, gratifying yourselves without restraint. You have fattened your hearts in preparation for a day of judgment.
    <verse eid="JAS 5:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="JAS 5:6"/>
    You have condemned and put to death the righteous one, and he does not resist you.
    <verse eid="JAS 5:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Patience in Suffering</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="JAS 5:7"/>
    Therefore, be patient, brothers and sisters, until the coming of the Lord. Consider how the farmer waits for the precious yield of the soil, patiently enduring until it receives both the early and the late rains.
    <verse eid="JAS 5:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="JAS 5:8"/>
    You also must be patient. Strengthen your hearts, because the coming of the Lord is near.
    <verse eid="JAS 5:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="JAS 5:9"/>
    Brothers and sisters, do not grumble against one another, so that you will not be judged. Look, the Judge is standing right at the door.
    <verse eid="JAS 5:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="JAS 5:10"/>
    Brothers and sisters, take as your example of suffering and patience the prophets who spoke in the name of the Lord.
    <verse eid="JAS 5:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="JAS 5:11"/>
    Look, we regard as blessed those who endured. You have heard of the perseverance of Job and have seen the outcome the Lord brought about, showing that the Lord is full of compassion and mercy.
    <verse eid="JAS 5:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Against Swearing</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="JAS 5:12"/>
    Above all, my brothers and sisters, do not swear oaths, either by heaven or by earth or by any other oath. Instead, let your “yes” truly mean yes and your “no” truly mean no, so that you may not fall under judgment.
    <verse eid="JAS 5:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Prayer and Singing Praises to God</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="JAS 5:13"/>
    Is anyone among you suffering hardship? He should pray. Is anyone experiencing joy? He should sing praises.
    <verse eid="JAS 5:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="JAS 5:14"/>
    Is anyone among you sick? He should call for the elders of the church, and they should pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of the Lord.
    <verse eid="JAS 5:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="JAS 5:15"/>
    And the prayer offered in faith will restore the one who is sick, and the Lord will raise him up. If he has committed sins, they will be forgiven.
    <verse eid="JAS 5:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="JAS 5:16"/>
    The prayer offered by a righteous person, when it is working, is remarkably powerful and effective in accomplishing God’s purposes.
    <verse eid="JAS 5:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="JAS 5:17"/>
    Elijah was a man with a nature like ours, and he prayed earnestly that it would not rain, and no rain fell on the land for three years and six months.
    <verse eid="JAS 5:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="JAS 5:18"/>
    Then he prayed again, and the sky gave rain, and the earth produced its harvest.
    <verse eid="JAS 5:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">On Straying from the Truth and Restoration</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="JAS 5:19"/>
    My brothers and sisters, if anyone among you strays from the truth and someone turns him back,
    <verse eid="JAS 5:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="JAS 5:20"/>
    let him know that the one who brings a sinner back from the error of his way will save his soul from death and will cover a multitude of sins.
    <verse eid="JAS 5:20"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="JAS 5"/>
</usx>
